      <div>
        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Americas</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Thule people</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">Coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a> and the Bering Strait, then the Canadian Arctic (Baffin Island, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hudson-bay">Hudson Bay</a>, Foxe Basin, the Mackenzie Delta) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a>; named after Thule (Pituffik) in north-west Greenland, where Comer’s Midden was dug.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">Forerunners: Old Bering Sea c. 200 BCE–500 CE, Punuk and Birnirk c. 800–1400 CE; eastward spread from c. 1000 CE; Classic Thule 1100–1400 CE; Post-Classic 1400–1600 CE, when local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inuit">Inuit</a> groups emerged.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">bowhead whaling from skin boats; dog sleds, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayak">kayaks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umiak">umiaks</a>; semi-subterranean houses framed with whale bone; polished-slate tools and the ulu knife; meteoric and Norse iron; the eastward migration that replaced the Dorset culture</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Excavated houses, middens and tools (the Fifth Thule Expedition of 1921–1924 and later digs); Norse sources, which call Arctic peoples Skrælingjar; genetic studies of Thule burials (2014) and of Arctic ancestry (2019).</span></div>
        </div>
        <p class="empire-meta">7 min read · Last reviewed October 2026</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of the Thule people</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li class="kid-tale-np"><p>About 1,000 years ago, the Thule people lived on the coasts of Alaska.</p><p>They were the ancestors of the Inuit and Yupik peoples.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/umiak.jpg" alt="Umiak" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Umiak <span class="kid-tale-cr">Gchacon at English Wikipedia, CC BY 2.5</span></figcaption></figure><p>They hunted bowhead whales from boats made of skins, with crews working together under a captain.</p><p>One whale gave meat for food, blubber for light and bone for houses and tools.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kayak.jpg" alt="Kayak" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Kayak <span class="kid-tale-cr">Ville Miettinen, CC BY 2.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>With dog sleds, kayaks and skin boats, they spread across northern Canada to Greenland.</p><p>There they took the place of the Dorset people, who had lived there before.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/copper.jpg" alt="Copper" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Copper <span class="kid-tale-cr">Jonathan Zander; derivative work by Materialscientist, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>They made knives of polished slate, and passed copper and iron from community to community.</p><p>Some iron came from meteorites, rocks from the sky, and some from trade with the Norse.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/inuit.jpg" alt="Inuit" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Inuit <span class="kid-tale-cr">Dragon401k, CC BY 4.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Later, a colder climate brought fewer whales, and the old whaling life broke up.</p><p>Their descendants are today’s Inuit and Yupik, and whaling captains are still important in Alaska.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Thule were the direct ancestors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inuit">Inuit</a> and Yupik, who developed in coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a> by 1000 CE and in a few centuries spread across the Canadian Arctic to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a>.</li><li>Bowhead whales were at the heart of Classic Thule life, hunted by cooperating boat crews under a captain, the umialik, and used for food, fuel, house frames and tools.</li><li>Dog sleds, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayak">kayaks</a> and large skin boats let the Thule range far and trade widely, and they displaced the earlier Dorset people across the eastern Arctic.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Long before European contact the Thule worked iron, some of it from meteorites such as the Cape <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> meteorite in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greenland">Greenland</a> and some traded from the Norse.</p></aside>
    </section>

